  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1217 PM CDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nDaily precipitation chances and \"cooler\" temperatures continue \nthrough the long term forecast. Through the rest of the work week, \nour forecast area remains between the two upper systems. However, \nthe upper trough across the Desert Southwest will gradually get \nabsorbed into northerly flow through the day Friday as a secondary \ntrough swings across the Pacific Northwest. Moist southerly flow \naloft will shift to moist southwesterly flow. Multiple passing \nshortwaves and subtropical moisture continuing to pump in over the \nregion, widespread chances for thunderstorms continue through the \nrest of the work week. Daily precipitation chances and cloud cover \nwill keep cooler temperatures with highs in the mid 70s to lower 90s \nboth Thursday and Friday. Over the weekend, we will see an upper \npattern change and a slight warm up in temperatures. Daily \nprecipitation chances remain in the forecast, however are much less \ncompared to this week. The upper ridging that was to the east of the \nforecast area will slightly shift south and expand back west across \nmuch of Texas and Mexico. Subtropical moisture will continue to fill \ninto the region around the upper ridging, however flow aloft will \nshift to the northwest. In general, northwesterly flow aloft and \nplenty of moisture will result in thunderstorms developing across \nhigher terrain in NM before expanding southeast into our region. \nThat is not the case with current models. Thunderstorm chances over \nthe weekend are mostly across southern regions of the forecast area \nwhere models indicate a shortwave will pass. However, if \nnorthwesterly flow does fill in aloft, I would not rule out higher \nterrain thunderstorms from NM. With the upper ridging just clipping \nthe forecast area, height and thickness increases will warm \ntemperatures up to the 90s for much of the region over the weekend.",
